i'd say the biggest challenge in terms  of filmmaking was  the underwater tunnels months of  training hours underwater and facing  intense fears were all part of a regular  day at work for the cast filming  underwater scenes for the harry potter  films i'm a terrible swimmer i'm really  really bad while he was filming the  underwater portion of the tri-wizard  tournament daniel radcliffe wound up  suffering two ear infections due to all  the time spent under the chilly water in  tests the team had tried to strap  radcliffe upside down in the water so  his hair would float up but it wound up  not being an ideal way to work so they  scrapped it since the giant filming tank  wasn't even heated it's no surprise the  actors weren't fans and radcliffe  suffered so many ear infections the  frozen part of the lake that harry  retrieves the sword of gryffindor from  in the deathly hallows was actually  plexiglas with frosted detailing to make  it look frozen from the top beneath they  had real water and when filming shots  looking upwards from under the lake the  top of the ice we see is actually melted  wax that floats on the water while the  underwater scenes look like they were  set in the bottom of a lake they were  actually filming everything in a giant  pool surrounded with blue screen  backgrounds so cgi could be done  afterwards that of course  was hugely challenging the pool was  built especially for the production it  was the deepest underwater tank for  filming in europe 20 feet deep by 60  square feet and able to hold an  impressive half a million liters of  water the massive tank had a filtration  system to manage the chlorine levels  keeping it more pure than mineral water  which helped in keeping the water clear  for filming and it made it much less  irritating for the actor's eyes when  doing these underwater shots the team  didn't plan to record more than around  10 seconds at a time that's as long as  you can do without air and keeping your  eyes open giving radcliffe the  opportunity to take a break and grab  some oxygen from a tank when he needed  it i'm free i'd blow all the air that i  could out of my lungs  not only did he have to swim and act  underwater radcliffe actually had to  speak but his dialogue wasn't recorded  while filming in the giant tank he had  to say the line but he's my friend trebu  and his mouth filled right up with water  but luckily it wasn't too rough of an  experience when it came time to record  his dialogue a much smaller crate was  filled with water and radcliffe would  put his face in it speaking into an  underwater microphone that was hooked up  in the tank  you're sure about this neville  absolutely for an hour more slightly  whenever the harry potter actor was  filming scenes after when he would have  consumed giliweed and needed to show off  gills and webbed feet an hour and a half  of time was spent in the makeup chair  with tricky to work with silicone  prosthetics the job had to be thorough  since the prosthetics needed to be extra  secure to stay in place underwater  amidst all the intense action as for the  filaments inside the gills silk material  wound up being used instead of any  prosthetic because of just how well the  fabric moves underwater  radcliffe's mind was blown as he  encountered and made use of the  underwater sanctuaries used when the  goblet of fire was filming he felt like  a fascinated five-year-old using the  underwater habitat which was essentially  a small tank underwater with windows and  a chimney but it allowed a person to  take off their mask and to be kept dry  and out of water for a minute between  takes the harry potter actor described  it as a low budget version of the big  brother diary room  radcliffe definitely was never on his  own underwater a 20-person diving team  helped make the magic happen in the tank  safely and efficiently one of them was  just there to make sure there weren't  any bubbles in the actor's face or hair  since it was so difficult to film in  these conditions small roles like the  divers in charge of air regulators and  every single camera crew member played a  significant part in making everything  run smoothly 42 hours is nearly the  amount of time radcliffe spent  underwater filming for the fourth movie  alone sometimes spending up to three  hours underwater at a time six months  were dedicated to scuba training alone  and six weeks for filming the scenes  we obviously wanted to get as much  practice in as possible for the  underwater stuff radcliffe's incredible  stunt double david holmes mapped out  what the course should be underwater for  the actor and went through the whole  routine with radcliffe until he knew it  well enough to perform a ton of cgi was  done to create different kinds of plants  to appear underwater as part of the dark  and dank environment they imagined it to  be deep in a giant scottish lock it was  so vast since the water was naturally so  clear they had to muddy up the quality  of visuals underwater consistently the  mer people were tricky because they  needed to look like mythical creatures  while still coming across as  intimidating each creature is designed  because virtually nothing is from the  real world they wound up looking like  deep water mer people with creepy  fish-like mouths the grindylows were  created with cgi but when actually  filming radcliffe was being tugged at by  two stuntmen in blue water suits who  were grappling at him to help him depict  the scene realistically the victor crum  actor had a huge phobia of deep water  and swimming but then he did harry  potter and the goblet of fire where he  had to face his fears you know when  you're breathing that oxygen underwater  yeah and you breathe it too too fast and  too heavily to help ioneski get over his  deepest fear the actor would practice in  the shower allowing the water falling  down from the shower head to overwhelm  him so he could channel being in the  giant tank robert pattinson had some  struggles as well becoming very  disoriented underwater and unaware of  which direction he was really looking in  and what scenes would look like on  camera main thing that really sort of  was a bit frustrating was the fact that  communication is obviously quite  difficult since radcliffe was underwater  every message or note the director had  was passed along through a chain making  the process far less efficient  underwater  it's not as easy as that you know it  also meant radcliffe had to be  completely concentrated while he was  working  i like to think i did okay but i'm not i  just can't be certain  every shot from the underwater scenes  began with a blue screen background that  was lit from behind and then saw the  environment formulated from scratch the  blue screen wound up being extremely  complex especially for the electricians  who had to deal with fluorescent tubes  and cables all around water nothing they  could do with special effects would look  as real as it does when you're doing it  on camera emma watson rupert grint and  daniel radcliffe all went through what  wound up being one of the most annoying  scenes to film together being dropped  into a freezing lake during the harshest  months of winter while normally they  could wear layers the actors needed to  change right after the lake drop meaning  they had to wear as little clothing as  we see them in on screen they didn't  have to do much acting all those shivers  are completely real rupert grint was  pretty preoccupied filming this frigid  scene believing his heart might have  stopped beating from the cold  underwater the language is pretty  different and radcliffe wasn't always  clear on the best communication  practices he once accidentally gave a  hand signal that meant  quick i'm drowning get me up to the  surface and sent everyone into a panic  with the crew acting quickly to save him  fortunately it wasn't the other way  around radcliffe had help whenever he  needed it for all his hard work  radcliffe was given a professional  diver's daily record book for christmas  i have did while filming and in the  total time i spent underwater was 41  hours and 38 minutes the actor was  extremely proud of his diving work and  the memento has the times and lengths of  each dive he made giving an amazing  detailed history of his journey the  whole dive crew also got a special  christmas photo photoshopped for maximum  holiday spirit it clearly took a lot of  work money and extremely skilled  professionals but the final result is  definitely worth it considering how  incredibly realistic all the underwater  scenes look  the actors had their work cut out for  them and rose to the challenge even  though it might have scared some of them  do you think you'd be able to do that  while working underwater
 

